From 0dc384ad204627cfcb71ff54898c1bc847ac7051 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:53:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Replace matplotlib.cm.get_cmap, removed in matplotlib 3.9

`matplotlib.cm.get_cmap` was deprecated in matplotlib 3.7 and deleted in 3.9.
`plt.cm.get_cmap(...)` now raises `AttributeError: module 'matplotlib.cm' has
no attribute 'get_cmap'`. Upstream capped `matplotlib<3.9` and the cap was
load-bearing; "Loosen dependency bounds" removed it on the grounds that
nothing in the code depended on the capped majors being absent, which was
wrong for this one API. With the recipe now solving matplotlib-base 3.11.1,
eight call sites raise on first use.

Note `plt.get_cmap` -- the pyplot-level function -- is a different name and
still exists in 3.11; only the `matplotlib.cm` one was removed. The other
matplotlib APIs deleted in 3.9/3.10/3.11 (`register_cmap`, `unregister_cmap`,
`matplotlib.docstring`, `cbook.is_string_like`, `dates.epoch2num`, `Axes3D`'s
`dist`/`w_xaxis`, unprefixed seaborn style names, `gca(**kwargs)`) are not
used anywhere in the package, so this is the whole tail.

The replacement is `matplotlib.colormaps[name]`, which matplotlib 3.5 added
and which the deprecation warning itself points at, so it works across the
entire `>=3.3` range the recipe allows.

The eight sites are not uniform, and the difference is silent:

* seven passed a count -- `get_cmap(name, n)` returned the colormap resampled
  to `n` entries, and they become `colormaps[name].resampled(n)`;
* `pose_estimation_pytorch/apis/visualization.py` passed no count, so the
  old two-arg form with `lut=None` returned the full 256-entry map. It
  becomes a bare `colormaps[cmap]`. Resampling it would not raise: that
  function indexes the result as `cmap(1)` -- an *integer* LUT index -- and
  on "rainbow" resampling to 3 moves that colour by 0.99 in a channel.

That site is already guarded by `if isinstance(cmap, str)`, so the fact that
`colormaps[...]` requires a string key where `get_cmap` also accepted a
`Colormap` changes nothing; it is the only site whose argument is not a
config string.

`modelzoo/generalized_data_converter/utils.py` used `plt` for nothing else,
so its function-local `import matplotlib.pyplot as plt` becomes a
function-local `from matplotlib import colormaps` rather than gaining one.

Colours are unchanged, checked rather than assumed. Comparing the RGBA that
`plt.cm.get_cmap` returns on matplotlib 3.7.3 against what the new
expressions return on 3.11.1 -- 14 colormaps across LinearSegmented and
Listed kinds, 11 resampling counts from 1 to 300, sampled on integer LUT
indices, a 1001-point float grid, and the under/over/bad values -- gives
1008 arrays that are bit-identical, with matching `N` and colormap class.
The same comparison run old-vs-new API within 3.7.3 alone is also identical,
which separates the API change from any version drift.

`tests/test_colormap_api.py` covers all eight sites. It asserts against RGBA
values captured from the removed API on 3.7.3, so it is an oracle from
outside this code, and it pairs two kinds of check because the two failure
modes need different ones:

* the removal is loud, so running a call site catches it -- but only on
  matplotlib >= 3.9. An AST check that each file resamples exactly where it
  used to is red on any matplotlib version, including one where the reverted
  code still runs.
* the resampling mistake is silent, so nothing but a colour assertion
  catches it. Resampling the un-counted site turns the AST check red without
  raising anywhere.

Reverting all eight hunks: 18 of the 21 new tests fail on matplotlib 3.11.1
and 9 fail on 3.7.3. The whole file passes on both.

`tests/test_path_str_boundaries.py::test_outlier_extraction_keeps_the_crop_whatever_type_the_video_is`
is red today on matplotlib 3.11 for this reason -- `outlier_frames.py:763`
reaches `utils/visualization.py`'s `get_cmap` helper -- and goes green here.
That helper is behind 15 of the indirect call sites; the other seven are in
GUI, 3D-plotting and PAF-visualisation paths the suite never enters.
