+"""The colormap lookups that ``matplotlib.cm.get_cmap`` used to serve.
+
+``matplotlib.cm.get_cmap`` was deprecated in matplotlib 3.7 and deleted in 3.9;
+``plt.cm.get_cmap(...)`` now raises ``AttributeError``.  Eight call sites used
+it, and every one of them sits in a plotting, GUI or video-labelling path that
+the test suite never executes, so the breakage reaches users rather than CI.
+
+Two failure modes have to stay covered, and they need different tests:
+
+* the *loud* one -- the call raises on matplotlib >= 3.9.  Any test that runs
+  the call site catches this, but only on a new enough matplotlib;
+* the *silent* one -- ``get_cmap(name, n)`` resamples the colormap to ``n``
+  entries while ``get_cmap(name)`` returns the full 256-entry map, so replacing
+  the second form with a resampled one changes the plotted colours without
+  raising anywhere.  ``apis/visualization.py`` uses the un-counted form and
+  then indexes the result with ``cmap(1)`` -- an *integer* LUT index -- where
+  resampling moves the colour most of the way across the map.
+
+So the colour assertions below compare against RGBA values captured from
+``plt.cm.get_cmap`` running on matplotlib 3.7.3, i.e. from the API that was
+removed.  They are an oracle from outside this code: they fail if the fix is
+reverted on a matplotlib that still has ``get_cmap``, they fail if a call site
+gains or loses a resampling, and they would fail if matplotlib ever changed the
+colormap data underneath us.
+"""
